+
+## Example
+
+Use natural units $c=\hbar=1$. Take the finite-dimensional scalar field
+space $V=\mathbb C^2$ with coordinates $(q,\widetilde q)$, standard
+Hermitian norm, compact gauge group $U(1)$ acting by
+$(q,\widetilde q)\mapsto(e^{i\alpha}q,e^{-i\alpha}\widetilde q)$, gauge
+coupling $g>0$, zero superpotential $W=0$, canonical scalar kinetic terms,
+and zero Fayet--Iliopoulos parameter. In the tree-level model the auxiliary
+maps are $F_q=F_{\widetilde q}=0$ and
+$D=-g(|q|^2-|\widetilde q|^2)$. The classical supersymmetric vacuum set is
+therefore
+$\{(q,\widetilde q):|q|=|\widetilde q|\}/U(1)$. The gauge-invariant
+coordinate $m=q\widetilde q$ gives a bijection of this orbit set with
+$\mathbb C$. This is a classical tree-level result and is not a claim about
+the quantum vacuum space.
+
+## Example references
+
+[Tong, §4.3.1, printed pp. 112–116](https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/supersymmetric-field-theory/susy.pdf).
+
+Classical F/D-flat quotient and quantum-moduli boundary: [[def-qftx-susy-vacuum-moduli-data]].
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** The U(1) action, canonical field data, $W=0$, and zero
+Fayet--Iliopoulos parameter in the Example.
+
+[A1] Adopt the finite-dimensional canonical rigid N=1 U(1) tree-level model stated in the Example; $q$ and $\widetilde q$ have mass dimension one and $g$ is dimensionless.
+
+[F1] [[def-qftx-susy-vacuum-moduli-data]]: classical vacua are the simultaneous zeros of the F and D maps, modulo the compact gauge group.
+
+## Verification
+
+**Given:** [A1] and the classical vacuum-moduli definition [F1].
+
+1.1 Since $W=0$, both holomorphic derivatives vanish identically, so every
+$(q,\widetilde q)$ is F-flat. The D equation is
+$-g(|q|^2-|\widetilde q|^2)=0$; because $g>0$, it is equivalent to
+$|q|=|\widetilde q|$. By [F1], the classical vacuum set is exactly that
+D-flat set modulo the displayed U(1) action. [A1, F1, given, algebra]
+
+1.2 The product $m=q\widetilde q$ is invariant under the action, since
+$(e^{i\alpha}q)(e^{-i\alpha}\widetilde q)=q\widetilde q$. It therefore
+defines a map from the D-flat orbit set to $\mathbb C$. [A1, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+2.1 For any $m_0\in\mathbb C$ with $m_0\ne0$, choose
+$q=\sqrt{|m_0|}>0$ and $\widetilde q=m_0/q$. Then
+$q\widetilde q=m_0$ and $|q|=|\widetilde q|=\sqrt{|m_0|}$; for
+$m_0=0$, choose $(q,\widetilde q)=(0,0)$. Thus every $m_0$ is attained by
+a D-flat pair. [step 1.1, construct, algebra]
+
+2.2 If two D-flat pairs have the same product $m_0=0$, their equal-modulus
+condition forces both pairs to be $(0,0)$. If $m_0\ne0$, both $q$-entries
+are nonzero and have modulus $\sqrt{|m_0|}$; hence their ratio is a phase
+$e^{i\alpha}$, and equality of products forces the corresponding
+$\widetilde q$-entries to differ by $e^{-i\alpha}$. The two pairs are
+therefore U(1)-gauge-equivalent. The map in step 1.2 is injective as well
+as surjective, proving the stated bijection. [step 1.2, step 2.1, algebra] ∎
