  | 
  | [A2] For a left-handed Weyl fermion, the chosen cubic-anomaly coefficient is $+1$ in the fundamental of $SU(N_f)_L$, $-1$ in its antifundamental, and zero in a singlet; independent copies contribute additively.
  | 
  | 
  | 
  | **Given:** [A1]–[A2] and the explicit electric/magnetic matter multiplicities in the Example.
  | 
  | 1.1 In the electric theory, each of the $N_c$ color components of $Q$ is a left-handed Weyl fermion in $\mathbf N_f$ of $SU(N_f)_L$, so these fields contribute $N_c$. The antiquarks and gaugino are $SU(N_f)_L$ singlets and contribute zero. Hence $\mathcal A_{\mathrm{electric}}=N_c$. [A1, A2, given, algebra]
  | 
  | 1.2 In the magnetic theory, the $\widetilde N_c$ color copies of $q$ are antifundamentals and contribute $-\widetilde N_c$. For each of the $N_f$ right-flavor components of $M$, its left-flavor index is fundamental, so the meson fermions contribute $+N_f$. The magnetic antiquarks and gaugino are left-flavor singlets. Therefore $\mathcal A_{\mathrm{magnetic}}=-\widetilde N_c+N_f$. [A1, A2, given, algebra]
  | 
  | 2.1 Substituting $\widetilde N_c=N_f-N_c$ gives $\mathcal A_{\mathrm{magnetic}}=-(N_f-N_c)+N_f=N_c =\mathcal A_{\mathrm{electric}}$. This verifies the stated cubic-anomaly match. [step 1.1, step 1.2, given, algebra]
  | 
  | 3.1 The calculation checks only this specified global-symmetry coefficient under the proposed field assignments. It supplies no map of all observables, no equivalence of renormalization-group flows, and no continuum construction; therefore it does not prove the proposed duality. [A1, step 2.1] ∎
